@unsafe(reason: "raw Vulkan one-call pixel readback facade", capabilities: [ffi])
fn vulkan_sffi_readback_u32_into(dest: [u32], handle: i64, pixel_count: i32, offset: i64) -> i64:
    """Download pixels into a CALLER-OWNED [u32] and return the readback
    identity checksum. Returns -1 on failure (a valid checksum is >= 0).

    Why this shape and not `-> ([u32], i64)`: returning a large array inside a
    TUPLE deep-copies its elements. Measured 800x600 (480,000 px) under JIT,
    that copy alone cost ~12.1 ms per frame -- larger than the download and
    the native fill combined (~3.6 ms). Passing the destination in and
    returning only a scalar keeps the array by-reference (assignment of the
    same array measures 22 us), and lets the caller reuse one buffer across
    frames instead of allocating 480,000 elements every frame.
    See doc/02_requirements/nfr/engine2d_vulkan_2d_perf.md."""
    if pixel_count <= 0:
        return -1
    if dest.len().to_i64() < pixel_count.to_i64():
        return -1
    if gpu_sffi_uses_interpreter_array_abi():
        val bytes = _vulkan_read_buffer_bytes_abi(handle, pixel_count.to_i64() * 4, offset)
        if bytes.len().to_i64() != pixel_count.to_i64() * 4:
            return -1
        var i: i64 = 0
        var checksum: i64 = 0
        while i < pixel_count.to_i64():
            val off = i * 4
            val px = bytes[off].to_i64() | (bytes[off + 1].to_i64() << 8) | (bytes[off + 2].to_i64() << 16) | (bytes[off + 3].to_i64() << 24)
            dest[i] = px.to_i32().to_u32()
            checksum = (checksum + px) % 2147483647
            i = i + 1
        return checksum
    rt_vulkan_readback_u32_checksum(dest, pixel_count.to_i64(), handle, offset)

@unsafe(reason: "raw Vulkan pixel-slot bulk copy facade", capabilities: [ffi])
fn vulkan_sffi_copy_u32_into(dest: [u32], src: [u32], count: i32) -> bool:
    """Bulk-copy `count` pixels from `src` into a CALLER-OWNED `dest`.

    The readback facade must hand its caller an array the backend does not
    retain, so it cannot simply return the retained host mirror. The
    element-at-a-time Simple loop that made that copy is O(pixels) interpreted
    work: measured ~482 ms per 7680x4320 frame and ~2x that at 8192x8192 --
    by itself more than half the frame, and larger than the GPU transfer it
    accompanies by two orders of magnitude. This routes the same copy through
    one native slot memcpy.

    Sized for the 8K defaults: there is no threshold and no chunking, because
    a single contiguous copy is exactly what the largest targets want and
    small framebuffers lose nothing by taking the same path. Returns false on
    any validation failure so the caller can fall back rather than consume a
    partially-written destination."""
    if count < 0:
        return false
    if count == 0:
        return true
    if dest.len().to_i64() < count.to_i64() or src.len().to_i64() < count.to_i64():
        return false
    if not gpu_sffi_uses_interpreter_array_abi():
        if rt_vulkan_copy_u32_slots(dest, src, count.to_i64()) == count.to_i64():
            return true
    # Fall back to the element loop on ANY native refusal. Externs that pass a
    # runtime array are rejected outright on the interpreter path, and
    # `gpu_sffi_uses_interpreter_array_abi()` does not predict that reliably:
    # whether a given method reaches the JIT or the interpreter is decided per
    # function, so a lane can answer "native ABI" and still execute this call
    # interpreted. Treating the refusal as a readback FAILURE returned an empty
    # framebuffer and made both pixel gates unable to compare anything. Correct
    # pixels on every lane come first; the speedup applies wherever the native
    # call is genuinely available.
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < count.to_i64():
        dest[i] = src[i]
        i = i + 1
    true

